Day#11-Eye Tracking & Foveated Rendering

Introduction--
--Eye tracking foveated rendering is a graphics trick that saves computer power,improves image clarity, and matches natural human vision 
by rendering high resolution only where you look. It tracks your eyes and blurs the outer edges.
--Eye tracking and foveated rendering are two differnt technologies.

                                                 Eye Tracking
                                                      ↓
                                         Knows where you are looking
                                                      ↓
                                              Foveated Rendering
                                    Uses that information to reduce GPU work

How it works--
--The Fovea Zone: The computer draws crisp, clear images directly in the tiny center spot of your eye focus.The Peripheral Zone: 
The computer draws blurry or low-detail images in the outer areas because your eyes cannot see fine details there anyway.Real-Time Tracking: Tiny cameras inside a headset
watch your pupils and shift the clear zone instantly as your eyes move.

Main Benefits--
--Less GPU Work: The graphics card processes far fewer total pixels, reducing system strain.Better Performance: Virtual reality games run faster, smoother,
and with fewer frame drops.Cooler Hardware: Lower workloads mean devices use less battery and run at cooler temperatures

Real World Example – Eye Tracking

-- Apple Vision Pro uses infrared cameras to track the user's eye movements, allowing users to select apps, navigate menus, and interact with the interface simply by looking at objects.

-- PlayStation VR2 uses eye tracking to improve gameplay interaction and enable eye-tracked foveated rendering, which helps increase graphics performance.

Real World Example – Foveated Rendering

-- PlayStation VR2 uses eye-tracked foveated rendering to render the area the user is directly looking at in high quality while rendering the peripheral areas at a lower resolution. 
This improves performance without noticeably reducing image quality.

